Quoting  Janis Papanagnou  <Janis_Papanagnou@hotmail.com>:
>You certainly mean "pass away"; is it possible for older players to
>"drift away" from a highly addictive game like Nethack?  :-}

Well, yes. These days I play Crawl and Jeff Lait's POWDER. I like NetHack,
but I've _played_ it, I've played every version from 3.1.3 dozens of
times, I've ascended every class. Short of the awkwardness of conducts
(and, well, I've never used Elbereth), what's next?

Also, I am increasingly aware of the slightly randomly accreted nature of
NetHack's gameplay, and the serious balance issues that gives rise to.
This isn't the "make the game harder so no-one else can ascend" position -
that's a complete straw man - but rather the observation that NetHack is
very hard until you learn a set of often simple techniques that make it
very easy, and that any attempt to rebalance the game without eliminating
those techniques will make things worse for inexpert players.

For example, alchemy lets you get hundreds of HP. If you boost monster
melee damage further, you make life miserable for people who don't know
about alchemy or don't exploit it to the hilt. The right answer there
is to cut off all the unlimited sources of HP and make it so knowing
about the limited sources grants an advantage worth having but not an
overwhelming one.

I want to be challenged in the middle and late game, and I'm not, as a
general rule. NetHack's like the curate's egg; there's some very good
bits, but a lot of stuff that needs a harder look, and I'd like to be able
to enjoy those good bits again.

<http://crawl.akrasiac.org/docs/crawl_manual.txt>; the Philosophy section.
This is a game that was designed, not accreted, and it shows.
